---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import { withBase } from '../lib/url';
---

<BaseLayout
  title="How it works"
  description="The contract idea: the LLM writes the creative layer, OELTKit provides the manifest, runtime, components, validation, and packager around it."
  path="/how-it-works/"
>
  <section class="section">
    <div class="container">
      <span class="eyebrow">How it works</span>
      <h1>The LLM writes the course. The toolkit signs the contract.</h1>
      <p class="lede">
        The creative layer is free-form HTML — whatever your AI assistant dreams up. OELTKit
        provides everything around it: the manifest that declares what the course is, the runtime
        that talks to your LMS, the components that make interactions accessible, and the packager
        that ships it. The LLM never has to get the plumbing right, because the plumbing isn't its
        job.
      </p>

      <ol class="arch" aria-label="Architecture: from your content to a shippable package">
        <li class="arch__box">
          <h2 class="arch__title">Your content</h2>
          <p>Free-form HTML and media, written by your AI assistant. The bespoke part stays bespoke.</p>
        </li>
        <li class="arch__box">
          <h2 class="arch__title">Components &amp; runtime</h2>
          <p>Accessible interaction components and one tracking API that speaks every LMS dialect.</p>
        </li>
        <li class="arch__box">
          <h2 class="arch__title">Validation</h2>
          <p>Accessibility, tracking, and packaging checks — findings the AI can read and fix itself.</p>
        </li>
        <li class="arch__box">
          <h2 class="arch__title">Package</h2>
          <p>A SCORM 1.2, SCORM 2004, or cmi5 zip that imports into the LMS you already have.</p>
        </li>
      </ol>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section section--rule">
    <div class="container">
      <span class="eyebrow">Walkthrough</span>
      <h2>The real artifacts, start to finish</h2>
      <p>
        This walkthrough shows the actual files at each step — the manifest, a content page, the
        validator's output (including a caught error), and the package landing in an LMS.
      </p>

      <div class="todo-note" role="note">
        <p>
          The manifest and page markup below use the real schema and component markup from the
          toolkit specs (<a href="https://github.com/oeltkit/oeltkit">oeltkit/oeltkit</a>); the
          validator output shows the actual <code>oelt validate --json</code> finding format. The
          one remaining placeholder is the LMS import screenshot, captured once a packaged course is
          imported into SCORM Cloud.
        </p>
      </div>

      <div class="walkthrough">
        <section class="walkthrough__step">
          <h3>1 · The manifest: <code>course.json</code></h3>
          <p>
            One declarative file: title, structure, completion rules, score weighting. Declared,
            not hand-wired — the runtime enforces it on every LMS target.
          </p>
          <div class="code-panel">
            <p class="code-panel__bar">course.json</p>
            <pre tabindex="0"><code>{`{
  "oelt": "0.1",
  "id": "com.example.data-privacy",
  "title": "Data Privacy Essentials",
  "lang": "en",
  "targets": ["scorm12", "scorm2004", "cmi5", "web"],
  "tracking": {
    "completion": { "rule": "required-interactions-passed" },
    "score": { "rule": "weighted-interactions", "mastery": 0.8 }
  },
  "structure": [
    { "id": "m1", "title": "Foundations", "pages": [
      { "id": "intro", "title": "Why privacy matters", "src": "pages/intro.html" },
      { "id": "quiz", "title": "Final quiz", "src": "pages/quiz.html",
        "interactions": [{ "id": "final-quiz", "type": "quiz", "required": true }] }
    ] }
  ]
}`}</code></pre>
          </div>
        </section>

        <section class="walkthrough__step">
          <h3>2 · A content page with a branching scenario</h3>
          <p>
            Plain HTML the LLM wrote, with an <code>&lt;oelt-branching&gt;</code> element where the
            decision point goes. No build step, no framework.
          </p>
          <div class="code-panel">
            <p class="code-panel__bar">lesson-2.html</p>
            <pre tabindex="0"><code>{`<section>
  <h1>Spot the phishing attempt</h1>
  <oelt-branching id="phish" start="n1">
    <script type="application/json">
      {
        "nodes": {
          "n1": {
            "text": "<p>An email asks you to reset your password via a link. You…</p>",
            "choices": [
              { "label": "Click the link", "to": "bad", "value": "click" },
              { "label": "Open the site yourself", "to": "good", "value": "direct" }
            ]
          },
          "good": { "text": "<p>Right — navigate there directly.</p>", "end": "passed" },
          "bad":  { "text": "<p>That link could be spoofed.</p>", "choices": [{ "label": "Try again", "to": "n1", "value": "retry" }] }
        }
      }
    </script>
  </oelt-branching>
</section>`}</code></pre>
          </div>
        </section>

        <section class="walkthrough__step">
          <h3>3 · Validation — including the part where it catches a mistake</h3>
          <p>
            The validator checks accessibility, tracking wiring, and package structure, and reports
            findings in a format the AI can act on. Honesty matters here: it will catch things, and
            that's the point.
          </p>
          <div class="code-panel">
            <p class="code-panel__bar">oelt validate · output</p>
            <pre tabindex="0"><code>{`$ oelt validate ./data-privacy --json
{
  "ok": false,
  "findings": [
    {
      "level": "error",
      "code": "interaction-missing-in-html",
      "message": "Declared interaction 'final-quiz' not found in page HTML.",
      "where": "module m1, page \\"Final quiz\\" (pages/quiz.html)",
      "message_human": "The page \\"Final quiz\\" declares an interaction \\"final-quiz\\", but pages/quiz.html has no element with that id. Add an element like <oelt-quiz id=\\"final-quiz\\">, or remove it from the manifest."
    }
  ]
}`}</code></pre>
          </div>
          <p class="walkthrough__note">
            Every finding carries a <code>message_human</code> sentence that names the page by
            title — so an AI assistant can read it and fix its own output, then re-validate.
          </p>
        </section>

        <section class="walkthrough__step">
          <h3>4 · The package, imported</h3>
          <p>
            <code>oelt package</code> produces a zip; the LMS sees a standards-compliant course. A
            screenshot of a real import lands here once the first verification run is done.
          </p>
          <div class="code-panel">
            <p class="code-panel__bar">LMS import</p>
            <pre tabindex="0"><code># TODO: screenshot of the packaged zip imported into SCORM Cloud
# (first verification target)</code></pre>
          </div>
        </section>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section section--rule">
    <div class="container">
      <span class="eyebrow">For developers</span>
      <h2>Want the long version?</h2>
      <ul>
        <li>
          <a href={withBase('/docs/')}>Architecture docs</a> — the full layer model behind the
          4-box picture above.
        </li>
        <li>
          <a href={withBase('/docs/tracking-guide/')}>Runtime &amp; tracking API</a> — how
          <code>oelt.track</code> maps to SCORM 1.2, SCORM 2004, and cmi5.
        </li>
        <li>
          <a href={withBase('/docs/mcp-setup/')}>MCP server &amp; tool list</a> — connect OELTKit
          to Claude Desktop, Claude Code, or any stdio MCP host.
        </li>
        <li>
          <a href={withBase('/docs/authoring-guide/')}>Authoring guide</a> — the manifest shape and
          how the content layer, components, and tracking fit together.
        </li>
      </ul>
    </div>
  </section>
</BaseLayout>

<style>
  .lede {
    font-size: var(--oelt-text-lg);
    color: var(--oelt-color-ink-soft);
  }

  .arch {
    display: grid;
    gap: var(--oelt-space-md);
    grid-template-columns: repeat(auto-fit, minmax(min(14rem, 100%), 1fr));
    list-style: none;
    counter-reset: arch;
    margin: var(--oelt-space-xl) 0 0;
    padding: 0;
  }

  .arch__box {
    counter-increment: arch;
    position: relative;
    background: var(--oelt-color-surface);
    border: var(--oelt-border-w-bold) solid var(--oelt-color-line-strong);
    border-radius: var(--oelt-radius-md);
    padding: var(--oelt-space-lg);
  }

  .arch__box::before {
    content: counter(arch, decimal-leading-zero);
    font-family: var(--oelt-font-mono);
    font-size: var(--oelt-text-xs);
    color: var(--oelt-color-accent);
  }

  .arch__box:not(:last-child)::after {
    content: '→';
    position: absolute;
    inset-inline-end: calc(-1 * var(--oelt-space-md));
    inset-block-start: 50%;
    translate: 0 -50%;
    color: var(--oelt-color-accent);
    font-family: var(--oelt-font-display);
    z-index: 1;
  }

  .arch__title {
    font-size: var(--oelt-text-lg);
    margin: var(--oelt-space-2xs) 0 var(--oelt-space-xs);
  }

  .arch__box p {
    font-size: var(--oelt-text-sm);
    color: var(--oelt-color-ink-soft);
    margin: 0;
  }

  .walkthrough {
    display: grid;
    gap: var(--oelt-space-xl);
    margin-top: var(--oelt-space-xl);
  }

  .walkthrough__step h3 {
    margin-bottom: var(--oelt-space-xs);
  }

  .walkthrough__note {
    font-size: var(--oelt-text-sm);
    color: var(--oelt-color-ink-soft);
    margin-top: var(--oelt-space-sm);
    max-width: var(--oelt-content-width);
  }

  .walkthrough .code-panel {
    max-width: var(--oelt-content-width);
  }

  .walkthrough pre {
    padding: var(--oelt-space-lg);
    font-size: var(--oelt-text-sm);
  }
</style>
